# stalk — success
#
# The stalk verb carried through: forced past the dice, the outcome reports SUCCESS.
#
# Reached in 1/12 of the sampled location ids, with REAL schedules (no --npc-static).
# That flag pins everyone to their workplace all day, which is exactly what this verb cannot have:
# it needs somebody who goes somewhere, and a pinned NPC stays put all day.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --location-type test --location-id 0 --start-area "Test Wood" --period morning --observe-only "farmer"

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

strategy succeed
goal stalk
advance 12 180
regions

click keyword 0
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
regions

click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect SUCCESS
# The OUTCOME, not just the banner. "SUCCESS" is the dice result — it is printed from
# result.Succeeded and says nothing about what the verb did, so a verb whose SuccessReports
# stopped producing anything would still pass every other assertion here. This is the chip
# the outcome block carries, and it is the only line that proves the verb had an effect.
expect Moved to:

# What makes this a stalk test rather than an any-verb test.
expect-verb stalk
# The outcome TYPE, asserted by its stable id rather than by chip wording. `expect <chip>`
# proves what the player was told; this proves what actually ran. See --outcome-audit.
expect-outcome area_move
quit
